  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Dutch core contribution to the Global Fund to fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria (GFATM), 2026-2029</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria (GFATM) was established in 2002 to mobilize, manage and invest resources to combat three of the world&#x2019;s deadliest infectious diseases. It has become one of the largest international financiers of programmes to combat these diseases, providing respectively 28%, 76% and 59% of all international funding for HIV, TB and malaria. This aligns with several objectives of the Dutch Global Health Strategy (2023-2030), and most specifically the objectives to improve access to primary health care (incl. HIV, TB, malaria and SRHR), and minimize cross-border health threats.

Since 2002, Global Fund&#x2013;supported programmes have helped save around 70 million lives by expanding access to essential HIV, TB and malaria services. The Fund exerts particular efforts to ensure that women and girls, LGBTQI people, children, youth and other vulnerable groups are included in efforts to make access universal. 
In 2024 alone, the GFATM contributed to:
* Providing antiretroviral treatment (ART) for 25.6 million people living with HIV;
* Treatment for 7.4 million people with TB; 
* The distribution of 162 million mosquito nets for malaria prevention. 

The NL-contribution of EUR 48,8 million per year (2026-2029) will support GFATM efforts to continue fighting HIV, TB and malaria. The GFATM&apos;s Investment Case for the fund&apos;s 8th Replenishment Round states that the Global Fund - if funded at the targeted USD 18 billion - is projected to save 23 million lives, avert 400 million infections and cut mortality due to HIV, TB and malaria by 64% between 2027 and 2029.</narrative>
  </description>
